    blurb: "Say yes and no politely, and pick the right \"no\" for the job: hindi, wala or huwag.",
    deck: "Traps for English speakers",
    body: `
<p>English uses "no" and "not" for almost everything. Tagalog has three different words, and each one has its own job: <b>hindi</b>, <b>wala</b> and <b>huwag</b>. Picking the right one is the real trap, so this lesson takes them one at a time.</p>
<h3>Yes: oo and opo</h3>
<p>To a friend, a sibling or a child, "yes" is <b>oo</b>. To anyone who gets <b>po</b> (an older person, a stranger, a shop assistant), say <b>opo</b>. Answering Lola with a plain <b>oo</b> sounds rude.</p>
{{ex:Oo.|OH-oh|Yes. (to a friend)}}
{{ex:Opo.|OH-pohq|Yes. (polite)}}
<h3>hindi: not</h3>
<p><b>hindi</b> means "not". It goes first, in front of a describing word, a noun or a verb, and a short pronoun such as <b>ako</b>, <b>ka</b> or <b>siya</b> moves up right after it. English says "I am not tired"; Tagalog says "not I tired". On its own, <b>Hindi.</b> is "No." To someone who gets po, say <b>Hindi po.</b></p>
{{ex:Hindi ako pagod.|heen-DEE ah-KOH pah-GOHD|I'm not tired.}}
{{ex:Hindi po.|heen-DEE pohq|No. (polite)}}
<p>Words like <b>alam</b> (know) keep their ng pronoun after <b>hindi</b>: <b>Hindi ko alam</b>, never <i>Hindi ako alam</i>.</p>
{{ex:Hindi ko alam.|heen-DEE koh ah-LAHM|I don't know.}}
<h3>wala: there's none, I don't have</h3>
<p>When the answer is "there isn't any" or "I don't have one", Tagalog does not use <b>hindi</b>. It uses <b>wala</b>. Someone asks <b>May tubig ba?</b> ("Is there water?") and there is none: the answer is <b>Wala.</b> To say who has none, add the pronoun with the linker <b>-ng</b>: <b>wala</b> + <b>ako</b> = <b>Wala akong…</b></p>
{{ex:May tubig ba?|mai TOO-beeg bah|Is there any water?}}
{{ex:Wala.|wah-LAHQ|There's none.}}
{{ex:Wala akong pera.|wah-LAH ah-KOHNG PEH-rah|I don't have any money.}}
<h3>huwag: don't</h3>
<p>For "don't" in an instruction, use <b>huwag</b>, never <b>hindi</b>. The person you are talking to comes next, joined by the linker: <b>huwag kang…</b> to a friend, <b>huwag po kayong…</b> to someone who gets po. <b>Huwag!</b> alone means "Don't!"</p>
{{ex:Huwag kang umalis.|hoo-WAHG kahng oo-mah-LEES|Don't leave. (to a friend)}}
{{ex:Huwag po kayong umalis.|hoo-WAHG poh kah-YOHNG oo-mah-LEES|Please don't leave. (polite)}}
<h3>Not yet: Hindi pa</h3>
<p>The little word <b>pa</b> means "yet". When someone asks <b>Kumain ka na ba?</b> ("Have you eaten?"), a very common Filipino greeting, the answer may be <b>Hindi pa.</b> ("Not yet.").</p>
{{ex:Hindi pa po.|heen-DEE pah pohq|Not yet. (polite)}}
<h3>Answering a "not" question</h3>
<p>A question like "Aren't you hungry?" is easy to misread in any language, and a bare <b>oo</b> or <b>hindi</b> can leave people unsure what you mean. Play it safe: give the whole answer.</p>
{{ex:Hindi, busog na ako.|heen-DEEQ boo-SOHG nah ah-KOH|No, I'm full.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using <b>hindi</b> for "there isn't any". <i>May tubig ba? Hindi.</i> → <b>May tubig ba? Wala.</b> And for "don't": <i>Hindi ka umalis!</i> → <b>Huwag kang umalis!</b></div>
`,
