    deck: "Food and drink",
    body: `
<p>English uses two different verbs for two ideas: "there is" (There's rice) and "have" (I have a dog). Tagalog uses one small word for both: <b>may</b>. Its opposite is <b>wala</b>, which you met in the last lesson.</p>
<h3>may + noun</h3>
<p><b>may</b> goes straight in front of the noun, with no marker in between: no <b>ang</b>, no linker. On its own, <b>may</b> + noun means "there is". Add <b>ba</b> and it becomes a question.</p>
{{ex:May tubig ba?|mai TOO-beeg bah|Is there any water?}}
{{ex:May adobo po kami.|mai ah-DOH-boh poh kah-MEE|We have adobo. (polite, at a restaurant)}}
<h3>Who has it</h3>
<p>To say who has something, put the owner after the noun. A pronoun comes in its ang form (<b>ako</b>, <b>siya</b>, <b>kami</b>); a name takes <b>si</b>.</p>
{{ex:May aso ako.|mai AH-soh ah-KOH|I have a dog.}}
{{ex:May aso si Ana.|mai AH-soh see AH-nah|Ana has a dog.}}
{{ex:May dalawang aso ako.|mai dah-lah-WAHNG AH-soh ah-KOH|I have two dogs.}}
<h3>mayroon: the longer word</h3>
<p><b>mayroon</b> means the same as <b>may</b>, but it works differently. The pronoun comes right after it, joined by the linker <b>-ng</b>, and then the noun. In everyday speech <b>mayroon</b> is usually said and written <b>meron</b>. <b>Mayroon.</b> or <b>Meron.</b> alone is the answer "Yes, there is."</p>
{{ex:Mayroon akong aso.|mai-roh-OHN ah-KOHNG AH-soh|I have a dog.}}
{{ex:Mayroon po.|mai-roh-OHN pohq|Yes, there is. (polite)}}
<p>Both <b>May aso ako</b> and <b>Mayroon akong aso</b> are correct and mean the same. Use whichever comes to you first; at a carinderia or a sari-sari store you will hear both all day long, from the staff and from the customers.</p>
<h3>wala: none</h3>
<p><b>wala</b> works like <b>mayroon</b>: a pronoun after it takes the linker. For "there's no…", link <b>wala</b> straight to the noun: <b>walang</b> + noun.</p>
{{ex:Wala akong kapatid.|wah-LAH ah-KOHNG kah-pah-TEED|I don't have any brothers or sisters.}}
{{ex:Walang tubig.|wah-LAHNG TOO-beeg|There's no water.}}
<p>Asked <b>May kanin ba?</b>, answer <b>Mayroon.</b> if there is rice and <b>Wala.</b> if there isn't. Never <b>Hindi.</b>: that is "not", not "none".</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> forgetting the linker after <b>mayroon</b> or putting a marker after <b>may</b>. <i>Mayroon ako kotse</i> → <b>Mayroon akong kotse</b>; <i>May ang tubig?</i> → <b>May tubig ba?</b></div>
`,
